1
00:00:07,860 --> 00:00:11,880
At two in the morning, Beaumont Row Pharmacy hummed.

2
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:16,846
The fridge for the insulin hummed, and so did the strip light over the dispensing

3
00:00:16,846 --> 00:00:17,446
bench.

4
00:00:18,060 --> 00:00:21,220
Frank Farnsworth had stopped hearing either of them years ago.

5
00:00:22,040 --> 00:00:25,520
He took the key from his lanyard and opened the controlled-drugs cupboard.

6
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:31,440
It was steel, bolted to the wall, with a door that always stuck at the last inch.

7
00:00:32,340 --> 00:00:36,000
He put his hip against it, as he did every night, and it gave.

8
00:00:37,220 --> 00:00:38,940
The register lay open on the bench.

9
00:00:39,860 --> 00:00:41,520
He ran his pencil down the page.

10
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:43,840
Diazepam, correct.

11
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:46,060
Oxycodone, correct.

12
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:48,240
Methadone, correct.

13
00:00:49,320 --> 00:00:53,740
He counted each box aloud, softly, because the habit kept him honest.

14
00:00:53,740 --> 00:00:56,540
His own voice was company at that hour.

15
00:00:57,180 --> 00:00:58,940
Then he reached the bottom shelf.

16
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:02,670
A single blister pack sat behind the tramadol,

17
00:01:02,670 --> 00:01:05,460
a flat white card with a pharmacy label on it.

18
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:09,020
Morphine sulphate, ten milligram tablets.

19
00:01:10,120 --> 00:01:11,780
The register said nine remaining.

20
00:01:12,680 --> 00:01:15,400
He tipped the pack under the light and counted the cells.

21
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:17,160
Eleven tablets.

22
00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:19,700
"No," he said.

23
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:24,180
He counted again, touching each cell with the end of his pencil.

24
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:26,480
Eleven.

25
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:27,680
Two more than the book.

26
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:30,600
Two more than he had any right to hold.

27
00:01:31,640 --> 00:01:32,960
Frank sat down on the stool.

28
00:01:34,020 --> 00:01:34,840
Shortages happened.

29
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:37,644
Everyone knew the cure for a shortage,

30
00:01:37,644 --> 00:01:40,620
and it was paperwork and a phone call to the superintendent.

31
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:43,600
Nobody had a cure for a surplus.

32
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:48,360
Surplus meant that something had come in, and nothing had come in.

33
00:01:48,360 --> 00:01:50,060
He read the label.

34
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:54,572
The print was the old type, from the printer they'd thrown out when the new system

35
00:01:54,572 --> 00:01:55,172
arrived.

36
00:01:55,720 --> 00:01:58,080
Patient: Mallory, Victor.

37
00:01:58,920 --> 00:02:00,540
Dispensed by: F.

38
00:02:00,940 --> 00:02:01,540
Farnsworth.

39
00:02:02,300 --> 00:02:04,380
The date was six years ago this month.

40
00:02:05,220 --> 00:02:06,860
He remembered nothing about the man.

41
00:02:07,520 --> 00:02:09,320
That bothered him more than it should have done.

42
00:02:10,080 --> 00:02:11,340
He remembered most scripts.

43
00:02:12,120 --> 00:02:16,600
He remembered the regulars and the odd ones, and the woman who always paid in coins.

44
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:20,200
But Mallory, Victor was only a name on a card.

45
00:02:21,160 --> 00:02:24,660
He went to the back office and found the old collection ledger.

46
00:02:25,420 --> 00:02:27,440
It was a ring binder with a green spine.

47
00:02:28,440 --> 00:02:30,720
He turned to the right month and found the line.

48
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:32,120
Mallory.

49
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:35,200
Morphine sulphate 10 mg.

50
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:35,920
Not collected.

51
00:02:36,580 --> 00:02:37,840
Stock returned to cupboard.

52
00:02:38,740 --> 00:02:40,540
His own initials sat beside it.

53
00:02:41,260 --> 00:02:43,500
So the pack had been there six years.

54
00:02:44,280 --> 00:02:46,900
Nine tablets, written off and forgotten.

55
00:02:47,680 --> 00:02:51,810
He had checked this cupboard on hundreds of nights and never once questioned the

56
00:02:51,810 --> 00:02:53,100
card behind the tramadol.

57
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:56,340
"Nine," he said, back at the bench.

58
00:02:57,020 --> 00:02:57,660
"It was nine."

59
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:02,880
He counted a third time, slowly, saying each number aloud.

60
00:03:03,840 --> 00:03:05,720
At eleven his voice stopped.

61
00:03:06,660 --> 00:03:08,700
Then he did what the regulations required.

62
00:03:09,460 --> 00:03:11,160
He wrote the figure he could prove.

63
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:17,598
He signed the register, Farnsworth, 2.07 a.m., and beside the line he wrote nine,

64
00:03:17,598 --> 00:03:21,020
because nine was the number that closed the book.

65
00:03:21,760 --> 00:03:25,327
Then, in smaller writing, he added eleven in brackets,

66
00:03:25,327 --> 00:03:27,440
and did not like the look of it.

67
00:03:28,100 --> 00:03:30,600
He lifted the pack to slide it back onto the shelf.

68
00:03:31,280 --> 00:03:33,200
That was when he saw the foil.

69
00:03:33,820 --> 00:03:35,860
One cell near the corner was empty.

70
00:03:36,760 --> 00:03:40,220
Its foil was sealed flat and silver, the way it should be.

71
00:03:40,960 --> 00:03:42,740
Only now it wasn't flat.

72
00:03:43,460 --> 00:03:47,480
It stood up very slightly in the middle, a faint blister of metal,

73
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:50,160
as if something beneath it was pressing out.

74
00:03:50,980 --> 00:03:52,360
Frank held the pack still.

75
00:03:53,240 --> 00:03:54,220
The fridge hummed.

76
00:03:54,860 --> 00:03:56,540
The foil did not move again.

77
00:03:57,340 --> 00:04:02,420
He put his thumb beside it, not on it, and found he was waiting to feel it breathe.

78
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:06,760
The next night, the count was twelve.

79
00:04:08,080 --> 00:04:09,900
Frank did not touch the pack at first.

80
00:04:10,820 --> 00:04:15,000
He stood at the cupboard with the door open and read the label from where he was.

81
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,500
Mallory, V.

82
00:04:18,380 --> 00:04:21,700
Morphine sulphate 10 mg.

83
00:04:21,700 --> 00:04:24,440
Twelve in the cells, and the register said nine.

84
00:04:25,060 --> 00:04:29,300
He had written nine himself, in ink, twenty-four hours before.

85
00:04:30,380 --> 00:04:31,700
He checked the lanyard.

86
00:04:32,380 --> 00:04:37,160
The key sat against his sternum, on its steel ring, where it had sat all shift.

87
00:04:37,940 --> 00:04:39,660
He had not taken it off to eat.

88
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:42,360
He had not taken it off to use the toilet.

89
00:04:43,720 --> 00:04:45,700
The following night it was thirteen.

90
00:04:46,680 --> 00:04:48,520
The night after that, fourteen.

91
00:04:49,620 --> 00:04:52,880
On the Thursday morning he rang Elsie Ebdon at home.

92
00:04:52,880 --> 00:04:56,840
She answered on the fifth ring, with a radio going behind her.

93
00:04:57,420 --> 00:04:58,020
"Frank.

94
00:04:58,640 --> 00:04:59,600
You're meant to be asleep."

95
00:05:00,780 --> 00:05:03,820
"Elsie, who else has a key to the CD cupboard?"

96
00:05:04,580 --> 00:05:05,180
"Me.

97
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:06,200
You.

98
00:05:06,320 --> 00:05:07,160
That's the list, love.

99
00:05:07,540 --> 00:05:08,140
You know that.

100
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:10,040
A kettle clicked off.

101
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:11,400
"Why?"

102
00:05:12,140 --> 00:05:14,200
"There's a pack in there that's gaining tablets."

103
00:05:15,160 --> 00:05:16,060
She laughed.

104
00:05:16,700 --> 00:05:20,080
It was a warm, easy laugh, and it made him feel foolish.

105
00:05:20,080 --> 00:05:22,200
"Gaining.

106
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:22,800
Frank.

107
00:05:23,240 --> 00:05:24,400
It's a stock transfer.

108
00:05:24,940 --> 00:05:27,752
We took that batch from the Alder Street branch in the spring,

109
00:05:27,752 --> 00:05:30,020
and somebody will have booked half of it in twice.

110
00:05:30,660 --> 00:05:31,480
I'll look at it Monday."

111
00:05:32,580 --> 00:05:34,140
"The label's six years old."

112
00:05:35,060 --> 00:05:36,700
"Then somebody's reused a card.

113
00:05:37,420 --> 00:05:38,020
Go home.

114
00:05:38,320 --> 00:05:38,920
Eat something."

115
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:40,440
She rang off.

116
00:05:41,500 --> 00:05:44,080
Frank stood in his kitchen with the phone in his hand.

117
00:05:44,960 --> 00:05:46,680
Elsie had not asked which pack.

118
00:05:47,400 --> 00:05:48,500
He had not told her.

119
00:05:49,420 --> 00:05:53,200
That night he came in at nine and went straight to the dispensing bench.

120
00:05:54,200 --> 00:05:58,940
He tore a sheet from the A4 pad, laid it flat, and set the pack on top of it.

121
00:05:59,720 --> 00:06:03,540
Then he drew round the blister card with a pencil, cell by cell.

122
00:06:04,460 --> 00:06:08,980
Fourteen tablets, fourteen small circles, each one shaded in.

123
00:06:09,720 --> 00:06:11,380
The empty cells he left clear.

124
00:06:12,220 --> 00:06:15,700
He wrote the time in the corner. 9.20 p.m.

125
00:06:16,320 --> 00:06:20,080
He put the sheet and the pack back in the cupboard, turned the key,

126
00:06:20,080 --> 00:06:22,100
and hung the lanyard round his neck.

127
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:24,960
He did not sit down all night.

128
00:06:25,860 --> 00:06:27,260
He answered the hatch twice.

129
00:06:28,240 --> 00:06:32,697
He filled a methadone script for a man who called him "chief" and smelled of wet

130
00:06:32,697 --> 00:06:33,297
dog.

131
00:06:33,820 --> 00:06:38,518
At every hour he went to the cupboard door and stood with his hand flat against the

132
00:06:38,518 --> 00:06:39,480
steel, listening.

133
00:06:40,340 --> 00:06:41,520
There was nothing to hear.

134
00:06:42,300 --> 00:06:43,360
The fridge hummed.

135
00:06:44,360 --> 00:06:47,800
At 2 a.m. he took the pack out and laid it on the sheet.

136
00:06:48,700 --> 00:06:50,600
It fitted the pencil lines exactly.

137
00:06:51,660 --> 00:06:54,500
Fourteen shaded circles, fourteen tablets.

138
00:06:55,460 --> 00:06:57,000
Then he saw the fifteenth.

139
00:06:57,920 --> 00:06:59,940
It sat in a cell he had drawn clear.

140
00:07:00,820 --> 00:07:06,060
He had left that circle white, and there it was, filled, white on white.

141
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,760
He picked the pack up and tilted it under the lamp.

142
00:07:10,580 --> 00:07:12,540
The tablet was ordinary in shape.

143
00:07:13,060 --> 00:07:15,000
But the stamp on its face had gone.

144
00:07:15,800 --> 00:07:19,765
The letters had been rubbed smooth, the way a coin goes in a pocket,

145
00:07:19,765 --> 00:07:21,980
or a step goes under a thousand shoes.

146
00:07:23,060 --> 00:07:24,160
Frank turned it over.

147
00:07:25,060 --> 00:07:26,440
The back was worn the same.

148
00:07:27,200 --> 00:07:29,820
Somebody had held this a very long time.

149
00:07:32,120 --> 00:07:36,030
Frank put the tablet down on the sheet, on its pencil circle,

150
00:07:36,030 --> 00:07:37,760
and did not touch it again.

151
00:07:39,200 --> 00:07:42,540
The dead-records room was a cupboard behind the consulting booth.

152
00:07:43,560 --> 00:07:45,720
It smelled of toner and old carpet.

153
00:07:46,680 --> 00:07:50,807
He took the lamp in there with him and pulled the lever-arch files for the year in

154
00:07:50,807 --> 00:07:51,407
question.

155
00:07:52,140 --> 00:07:53,080
Six years back.

156
00:07:54,040 --> 00:07:56,869
The prescription had a number on the pack label,

157
00:07:56,869 --> 00:08:00,523
and the number led him to a green box file marked UNCOLLECTED,

158
00:08:00,523 --> 00:08:02,940
with a rubber band gone brittle round it.

159
00:08:03,780 --> 00:08:04,900
The script was on top.

160
00:08:05,800 --> 00:08:07,020
He read it standing up.

161
00:08:08,200 --> 00:08:09,140
Victor Mallory.

162
00:08:10,020 --> 00:08:15,360
Morphine sulphate, 10 mg, one tablet every twelve hours for seven days.

163
00:08:15,360 --> 00:08:17,480
Fourteen tablets in all.

164
00:08:18,360 --> 00:08:20,320
The prescriber's stamp was from the hospice.

165
00:08:21,120 --> 00:08:24,414
Under the patient's name, in the address box,

166
00:08:24,414 --> 00:08:28,660
someone had written: Night Caretaker's Flat, Beaumont Row.

167
00:08:28,660 --> 00:08:30,180
Frank read that line twice.

168
00:08:31,100 --> 00:08:32,840
Beaumont Row was this building.

169
00:08:33,520 --> 00:08:35,040
The pharmacy took the ground floor.

170
00:08:35,500 --> 00:08:38,880
The flat was above it, and had been empty since before he started.

171
00:08:39,720 --> 00:08:41,620
The date on the script was a Tuesday.

172
00:08:42,460 --> 00:08:45,120
He remembered nothing of it, and that bothered him.

173
00:08:45,720 --> 00:08:46,960
He remembered most things.

174
00:08:47,860 --> 00:08:52,880
Under the script, folded small, was a death notice cut from the parish bulletin.

175
00:08:53,800 --> 00:08:59,480
Victor Mallory, aged sixty-eight, for eleven years night caretaker at Beaumont Row.

176
00:09:00,180 --> 00:09:01,760
Died on the Friday of that week.

177
00:09:02,640 --> 00:09:05,404
Someone had pinned it to the script with a staple,

178
00:09:05,404 --> 00:09:08,500
and the staple had rusted a brown ghost onto both pages.

179
00:09:09,480 --> 00:09:12,580
He went back to the bench and laid the pack beside the script.

180
00:09:13,400 --> 00:09:14,840
He worked it through with a pencil.

181
00:09:16,060 --> 00:09:18,340
The course began with two tablets a day.

182
00:09:19,020 --> 00:09:23,200
The pack had given him one, then two, then a pair on the second night.

183
00:09:24,160 --> 00:09:25,900
The pack was not growing at random.

184
00:09:26,600 --> 00:09:30,560
It was keeping to the script, dose by dose, and in the right cells.

185
00:09:30,560 --> 00:09:32,420
Fifteen tablets.

186
00:09:33,380 --> 00:09:35,220
Day eight of fourteen would be next.

187
00:09:36,140 --> 00:09:38,280
He rang Elsie at ten to three.

188
00:09:38,280 --> 00:09:41,020
She picked up on the sixth ring, thick with sleep.

189
00:09:41,780 --> 00:09:42,380
"Frank?

190
00:09:42,880 --> 00:09:43,620
What's happened?"

191
00:09:44,540 --> 00:09:45,320
"Nothing's happened.

192
00:09:45,980 --> 00:09:47,060
I need to ask you something.

193
00:09:47,880 --> 00:09:51,500
Who cleared out the old basement dispensary, when the building was refitted?"

194
00:09:52,400 --> 00:09:54,120
There was a long gap on the line.

195
00:09:55,060 --> 00:09:56,180
He could hear her breathing.

196
00:09:56,960 --> 00:10:00,640
Then a lighter click, though she had given up smoking years ago.

197
00:10:01,340 --> 00:10:02,560
"Why do you want to know that?"

198
00:10:03,360 --> 00:10:05,180
"Because Victor Mallory lived upstairs.

199
00:10:05,880 --> 00:10:06,860
You must have known him."

200
00:10:07,500 --> 00:10:08,180
"I didn't.

201
00:10:08,180 --> 00:10:09,780
She said it flatly.

202
00:10:10,440 --> 00:10:13,260
"Frank, I've managed this branch eleven years.

203
00:10:13,840 --> 00:10:15,940
I've signed every day sheet in that drawer.

204
00:10:16,600 --> 00:10:19,380
I have never once seen Mr Mallory's name on one."

205
00:10:20,080 --> 00:10:21,240
"Then he wasn't on days."

206
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:22,600
"No.

207
00:10:22,880 --> 00:10:23,800
He's on the night ones.

208
00:10:24,620 --> 00:10:25,240
Another pause.

209
00:10:26,180 --> 00:10:27,800
"In a hand that isn't mine, Frank.

210
00:10:28,420 --> 00:10:29,020
Or yours.

211
00:10:29,700 --> 00:10:32,100
I always took it for the locum's."

212
00:10:32,100 --> 00:10:35,700
Frank looked at the night register, shut on the bench under the lamp.

213
00:10:36,600 --> 00:10:39,880
"Elsie," he said, "what does the hand look like?"

214
00:10:40,740 --> 00:10:41,640
She did not answer.

215
00:10:42,500 --> 00:10:46,780
Behind him, very faintly, the cupboard door ticked against its frame.

216
00:10:48,480 --> 00:10:50,380
"What does it look like?

217
00:10:50,940 --> 00:10:51,740
Frank said again.

218
00:10:52,720 --> 00:10:55,860
"I'm not doing this at three in the morning," said Elsie.

219
00:10:56,460 --> 00:10:57,060
"Go home.

220
00:10:57,480 --> 00:10:58,140
Leave the cupboard."

221
00:10:58,880 --> 00:10:59,980
"I can't leave it.

222
00:11:00,360 --> 00:11:01,380
I'm the one who's on."

223
00:11:02,120 --> 00:11:03,460
She put the phone down.

224
00:11:03,460 --> 00:11:06,480
He heard the line click and then go flat.

225
00:11:07,360 --> 00:11:10,980
He took the night register from the bench and opened it under the lamp.

226
00:11:11,840 --> 00:11:13,900
It was the long ledger with the green spine.

227
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:16,000
Each night had a row.

228
00:11:16,800 --> 00:11:22,820
Date, opening count, closing count, signature of pharmacist, signature of checker.

229
00:11:23,560 --> 00:11:24,960
He turned back through it.

230
00:11:25,640 --> 00:11:26,260
Last week.

231
00:11:26,960 --> 00:11:27,600
Last month.

232
00:11:28,320 --> 00:11:28,980
Last year.

233
00:11:29,860 --> 00:11:34,700
His own name sat in the pharmacist column, night after night, in blue ink.

234
00:11:35,420 --> 00:11:39,460
In the column beside it, the checker's column, there was a second hand.

235
00:11:40,060 --> 00:11:41,200
Small and upright.

236
00:11:41,940 --> 00:11:44,660
The pencil had been pressed hard enough to dent the page.

237
00:11:45,500 --> 00:11:46,100
V.

238
00:11:46,180 --> 00:11:46,780
Mallory.

239
00:11:47,480 --> 00:11:49,160
He had never worked with a checker.

240
00:11:49,700 --> 00:11:50,360
Nobody had.

241
00:11:51,040 --> 00:11:53,127
The pharmacy ran one person on nights,

242
00:11:53,127 --> 00:11:57,411
and he had always taken the second signature to be a formality that the system

243
00:11:57,411 --> 00:11:58,011
filled in.

244
00:11:58,600 --> 00:11:59,940
He had never looked at it.

245
00:12:00,720 --> 00:12:01,860
He went back further.

246
00:12:02,560 --> 00:12:03,160
Two years.

247
00:12:03,680 --> 00:12:04,280
Four.

248
00:12:04,720 --> 00:12:05,320
Six.

249
00:12:05,980 --> 00:12:07,460
The hand did not change.

250
00:12:08,240 --> 00:12:08,720
V.

251
00:12:08,720 --> 00:12:10,751
Mallory, on every one of his shifts,

252
00:12:10,751 --> 00:12:13,740
from the first night he had ever been given the keys.

253
00:12:14,800 --> 00:12:18,727
Frank took the sheet with the pack's cells drawn on it and laid it open on the

254
00:12:18,727 --> 00:12:19,327
register.

255
00:12:19,900 --> 00:12:22,084
Then he turned to the last page of the book,

256
00:12:22,084 --> 00:12:24,120
where the checker had a margin for notes.

257
00:12:24,780 --> 00:12:26,260
There were pencil lines there.

258
00:12:26,900 --> 00:12:28,840
Ruled boxes in rows of fourteen.

259
00:12:29,600 --> 00:12:31,840
Dots in some of them, none in others.

260
00:12:32,820 --> 00:12:34,560
He held the sheet beside the margin.

261
00:12:35,420 --> 00:12:36,420
The dots matched.

262
00:12:36,420 --> 00:12:40,989
Every one he had pencilled, in the same cells, in the same order,

263
00:12:40,989 --> 00:12:43,520
and three that he had not drawn yet.

264
00:12:44,340 --> 00:12:45,420
He sat for a while.

265
00:12:46,160 --> 00:12:47,120
The lamp hummed.

266
00:12:47,780 --> 00:12:49,880
Out in the shop the fridge cycled on.

267
00:12:51,120 --> 00:12:54,720
Then he understood it, and it was plainer than he wanted.

268
00:12:55,620 --> 00:12:57,160
The pack was not filling.

269
00:12:57,940 --> 00:12:59,120
It had never been filling.

270
00:12:59,860 --> 00:13:04,543
Somebody had dispensed a course six years ago and it had never been handed over,

271
00:13:04,543 --> 00:13:06,240
so the count could not close.

272
00:13:07,160 --> 00:13:08,840
The tablets were not arriving.

273
00:13:09,640 --> 00:13:12,038
They were being brought back, one at a time,

274
00:13:12,038 --> 00:13:15,200
to the only person who stood at that cupboard every night.

275
00:13:15,960 --> 00:13:19,565
He picked the pack up from the bench, where it had lain beside the script,

276
00:13:19,565 --> 00:13:20,880
and put it in the cupboard.

277
00:13:21,820 --> 00:13:24,060
He turned the key, and it turned easily.

278
00:13:24,840 --> 00:13:27,500
Then he took it out again and held it in his palm.

279
00:13:28,420 --> 00:13:32,180
The foil over the ninth cell was bulged, a little more than before.

280
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:37,914
In the drawer under the bench was the collection slip, still clipped to the script,

281
00:13:37,914 --> 00:13:40,400
the box at the bottom blank for six years.

282
00:13:41,300 --> 00:13:42,200
He took his pen.

283
00:13:42,900 --> 00:13:45,160
His hand was steady, which surprised him.

284
00:13:46,100 --> 00:13:50,140
"If I sign," he said, to the empty shop, "it's closed."

285
00:13:51,320 --> 00:13:52,060
Nothing answered.

286
00:13:52,800 --> 00:13:55,320
He wrote: received, F.

287
00:13:55,460 --> 00:13:58,140
Farnsworth, 3.05 a.m.

288
00:13:58,140 --> 00:14:01,540
Then he signed his name under it and pressed the pen down hard.

289
00:14:02,500 --> 00:14:06,840
The cupboard door, which he had left open, swung slowly shut behind him.

290
00:14:08,560 --> 00:14:11,120
The shop was very quiet after that.

291
00:14:11,820 --> 00:14:14,640
Frank stood with the pack in his hand and listened.

292
00:14:15,560 --> 00:14:17,160
The fridge had cycled off.

293
00:14:17,920 --> 00:14:21,300
The lamp had stopped humming, or he had stopped hearing it.

294
00:14:22,220 --> 00:14:25,800
He went to the register on the bench and opened it to the night's page.

295
00:14:26,800 --> 00:14:28,960
His own handwriting sat above the line.

296
00:14:29,740 --> 00:14:31,220
It gave the count of nine.

297
00:14:32,260 --> 00:14:37,560
Beneath it, in pencil that was not his, was the count of eleven, struck through.

298
00:14:38,340 --> 00:14:38,960
Then twelve.

299
00:14:39,880 --> 00:14:43,540
Then the figure he had been dreading, and it stood there plain.

300
00:14:44,460 --> 00:14:45,060
Fourteen.

301
00:14:45,940 --> 00:14:49,300
A full course, and the line ruled off beneath it.

302
00:14:50,060 --> 00:14:50,660
It balanced.

303
00:14:51,380 --> 00:14:55,391
He had wanted that for four nights, and now he had it,

304
00:14:55,391 --> 00:14:57,620
and he did not want it at all.

305
00:14:58,360 --> 00:15:00,340
He looked at the clock over the door.

306
00:15:00,340 --> 00:15:02,940
Three-oh-six.

307
00:15:02,940 --> 00:15:04,720
"That's that," he said.

308
00:15:05,460 --> 00:15:07,120
His voice sounded thin in the room.

309
00:15:07,760 --> 00:15:08,580
"It's closed."

310
00:15:09,660 --> 00:15:10,560
Nobody answered.

311
00:15:11,460 --> 00:15:13,300
He walked the pack back to the cupboard.

312
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:15,280
The door had shut itself.

313
00:15:16,260 --> 00:15:20,593
He took the lanyard from his neck and found the key, and he turned the key,

314
00:15:20,593 --> 00:15:23,540
and the door opened with its usual small complaint.

315
00:15:24,420 --> 00:15:26,100
He raised the pack to the shelf.

316
00:15:26,100 --> 00:15:28,120
Then he read the label.

317
00:15:28,840 --> 00:15:30,760
He had not read it again since the slip.

318
00:15:31,280 --> 00:15:32,280
There had been no need.

319
00:15:32,940 --> 00:15:35,040
The printing was the same plain black.

320
00:15:35,640 --> 00:15:37,700
The pharmacy name, Beaumont Row.

321
00:15:38,320 --> 00:15:42,720
The drug, the strength, the quantity of fourteen.

322
00:15:42,720 --> 00:15:44,640
But the name above it had changed.

323
00:15:45,400 --> 00:15:47,520
It said Farnsworth, Frank.

324
00:15:48,380 --> 00:15:51,660
Beneath that was his date of birth, correct to the day.

325
00:15:52,440 --> 00:15:56,800
The instructions were the old instructions, one tablet every twelve hours.

326
00:15:57,380 --> 00:16:01,135
And at the bottom, where a dispenser prints the start of a course,

327
00:16:01,135 --> 00:16:02,500
it gave tomorrow's date.

328
00:16:03,360 --> 00:16:04,320
He did not shout.

329
00:16:05,100 --> 00:16:06,540
He would have liked to say he did.

330
00:16:07,240 --> 00:16:10,690
He stood there with the pack at shoulder height and read it twice,

331
00:16:10,690 --> 00:16:13,357
because he could not believe a printer had done it,

332
00:16:13,357 --> 00:16:15,500
and there was no printer in the cupboard.

333
00:16:16,400 --> 00:16:19,004
Then he closed the door on it, and locked it,

334
00:16:19,004 --> 00:16:21,260
and put the key back inside his collar.

335
00:16:22,160 --> 00:16:24,000
He thought of calling Elsie.

336
00:16:24,780 --> 00:16:27,780
He thought of the telephone and her voice going quiet.

337
00:16:28,460 --> 00:16:29,300
He did not call.

338
00:16:29,960 --> 00:16:31,260
What would he have said to her?

339
00:16:31,740 --> 00:16:32,600
That he had signed?

340
00:16:33,140 --> 00:16:37,380
She would only have told him what he already knew, that nobody else had.

341
00:16:37,980 --> 00:16:39,360
The door was open again.

342
00:16:40,040 --> 00:16:44,431
It had swung out an inch, and the pack lay on the shelf where he had not put it,

343
00:16:44,431 --> 00:16:45,200
label outward.

344
00:16:45,840 --> 00:16:46,680
He took it out.

345
00:16:47,260 --> 00:16:49,000
He did not remember deciding to.

346
00:16:49,680 --> 00:16:53,337
He carried it to the dispensing bench and sat down on the stool,

347
00:16:53,337 --> 00:16:58,080
the one with the torn vinyl, which Victor Mallory must have sat on for six years of

348
00:16:58,080 --> 00:16:58,680
nights.

349
00:16:59,240 --> 00:17:00,200
It was still warm.

350
00:17:00,860 --> 00:17:02,880
The foil on the first cell had bulged.

351
00:17:03,520 --> 00:17:07,944
It strained very slightly, as though something were pressing from beneath,

352
00:17:07,944 --> 00:17:09,080
patient and polite.

353
00:17:09,840 --> 00:17:12,780
He slid his thumbnail under the edge and tore it back.

354
00:17:13,460 --> 00:17:16,340
It came out into his open palm without being shaken.

355
00:17:17,020 --> 00:17:18,760
One small white tablet.

356
00:17:19,540 --> 00:17:21,040
He turned his hand to the lamp.

357
00:17:21,620 --> 00:17:25,854
The stamp on it was worn clean away, smooth as a pebble from a river,

358
00:17:25,854 --> 00:17:28,800
as if a great many hands had held it before his.

359
00:17:29,520 --> 00:17:33,940
Behind him, in the dark of the shop, the cupboard key turned in the lock.

